  

<h2 id="how-two-iphone-apps-help-me-harness-the-difference">How two iPhone apps help me harness the difference</h2>
<p>You are here, your deadline is there. It’s time to cross the space between those two points. How do you visualise that intervening time — as counting down, or counting up? As something that is running out, or something that is being filled?</p>
<p>I’d never considered these two ways of viewing time as being significant, but to my grandchildren (and to me) they absolutely are. Here’s how I know.</p>
<h2 id="the-goal-getting-to-the-car-on-time">The goal: getting to the car on time</h2>
<p>As the adult in charge I had forty five minutes to help two 9 year-olds and one 6 year-old tidy their rooms, pack their bags, get shoes on, and be in the car by a set time. As independently as possible.</p>
<p>Nothing about those tasks was difficult, but the children were in the middle of a game and needed a way to grasp how much time they were dealing with. Without me micro-managing the situation!</p>
<p>Yes, they can tell the time. But we needed a <em>timer</em>, not just a clock. And preferably one that showed the passing of time in a visual way. I have two such apps on my phone, each taking a different approach.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_177bc256f0ee469c998e912e20ece048/_image_cache/13ed4bdb-c9d5-4061-a444-230300fb577c/pasted-percent20image-percent2020260907124047.png" alt="Two iPhone screenshots of an app with a circle in the middle of the screen. Different fractions of the circle are filed with red in each screenshot: the one on the left has 24 minutes (of a 25 minute timer) to go, while the one on the right has 7 minutes remaining." width="1542" height="1522"></p>
<h3 id="app-1-time-timer-time-is-running-out">App 1: Time Timer (time is running out)</h3>
<p>The first is <a href="https://www.timetimer.com/collections/applications" target="_blank">Time Timer</a>, or the<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>pizza”, as one little boy calls it. It’s a red circle that gradually disappears in the same way a pizza disappears when slices are taken from it. When time is up, no pizza remains. I also have a physical version of this timer stuck magnetically to my oven. It gives at a glance, from anywhere in the room, a pretty good idea of how much cooking time is left.</p>
<p>I’ve chosen an applause alarm over the standard beep-beep-beep.</p>
<p>This was the aid I thought we needed to get my grandchildren in the car on time, so imagine my surprise when things fell apart! I quickly learned that for these particular children, at this particular time, seeing something run out was stressful. Instant shut down.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_177bc256f0ee469c998e912e20ece048/_image_cache/ba39eeee-d8e9-4688-9fb9-21d3a5ee0633/pasted-percent20image-percent2020260907124452.png" alt="Two app screenshots showing blue water waves on white backgrounds at different positions on the screen" width="1518" height="1500"></p>
<h3 id="app-3-liquid-timer-time-is-filling-up">App 3: Liquid Timer (time is filling up)</h3>
<p>So we tried the other app: Liquid Timer. It begins with a white screen, with what looks like water lapping at the bottom. As time passes, the water slowly fills up the screen until no white remains. <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/visual-countdown-timer-liquid/id1271515042" target="_blank">The name of the app seems to have changed</a> since I downloaded it a few years ago. Here’s an <a href="https://visualtimer.com/timers/liquid-timer/" target="_blank">online version</a>, just for fun.</p>
<p>—Success!</p>
<p>The children kept playing their game, looking up every now and then to see what the water was doing. When it was about half full they excitedly ran to do their chores, making many trips back to the kitchen to see where the water was up to. I loved that they felt in control of the situation.</p>
<p>And I’ve learned that what works for kids, sometimes works for adults too.</p>
<h2 id="counting-up-for-low-pressure-tasks">Counting up for low-pressure tasks</h2>
<p>The other evening I went to clean out the fridge, and decided to help me focus on the task by setting a timer. Liquid Timer (counting up) suited that job perfectly. No pressure, just a fun way to keep on track. I especially like how this timer keeps counting after the screen has filled with water. It’s great feedback for low-pressure tasks.</p>
<p>Time Timer still has its place. I reserve it for situations where time running out means something significant, but only when I feel up to that kind of pressure. Because pressure is what you feel as the pizza slowly disappears, slice by slice! And sometimes that is exactly the kick up the butt some of us need.</p>
<p>But it didn’t work in the aforementioned case with my grandchildren. Perhaps it will work next time, when the mood has shifted and they are seeing things differently.</p>
<h2 id="you-cant-manage-time-just-the-way-you-look-at-it">You can’t manage time, just the way you look at it</h2>
<p>Abundance (time is filling up), or scarcity (time is running out)? Steven Covey’s influence caused me to internalise the notion that the former is good and the latter is bad.</p>
<p>But I say that when it comes to the way you see and use time, there’s room for both.</p>
<p>It’s not about whether you are a counting-down or counting-up kind of person. Each approach is a tool you can use to serve your current mindset. Right now I’m enjoying the perspective of filling my time with good things, like family and sunsets, the feeling of crisp new sheets on the bed and time spent really listening to the person in front of me.</p>
